## THE DECISIONS OF THIS ROUND (each Tuesday's default as coordinator; each a Question at the stamp; each a behaviour with a cell)
- **D21 A WAITER USES THE REFRESH IT WAITED FOR (Tuesday's ruling 1; Q21).** `DeviceEntry.Flights` counts COMPLETED flights: it is incremented where a flight ends with a result, under the entry's lock (success: fixed `DeviceTokenHolder.cs:390`, beside the access value it hands out; failure: `Fail`, fixed `:235`), and no longer at a flight's start (HEAD1 `:290` removed). An `AccessLease` carries the count of completed flights when it was handed out. A lazy caller records the count when it finds no access value (unchanged code, HEAD1 `:188`); after its wait, `if (entry.Flights != observed)` (fixed `:280`) means a flight completed after it looked: it throws that flight's failure (`LastOutcome == Failed`) or uses the access value it left. A flight abandoned by its caller's cancellation is not counted (its waiters refresh for themselves, round 1's rule). The 401 path is unchanged in shape (`RenewAsync` clears the refused value only when it is still the current one; T04 and B1869 G15 hold). HEAD1's `&& entry.LastOutcome != FlightOutcome.Abandoned` is dropped from the reuse test (DERIVED: an abandoned flight no longer moves the count, and a flight starts only when no access value is held, so the clause decides nothing). **Alternative (the reader's second sketch):** on the lazy path, after taking the flight lock, use `entry.Access` when it is not null (cost: a late caller after a FAILED flight refreshes again instead of reusing the failure, against ruling 1's "or its failure", and B1869 F3's 2 x `RefreshTimeout` wait stays for a timing-out endpoint). Cells T12 x3, T13 x2.
- **D22 `Forget` DURING A REFRESH STORES NOTHING (Tuesday's ruling 2; Q22).** `DeviceEntry` gains `Generation`. `DeviceTokenHolder.Forget` takes the entry (creating it if unseen) and, UNDER its lock: `Generation++` (fixed `:101`), clears the access value, `Ready`, and deletes the vault record (a vault failure is still `VaultFailed`). A flight captures `Generation` when it starts (fixed `:294`); when the endpoint has answered, it takes the entry's lock, and if the generation moved (fixed `:356`) it zeroes the new access value and ends `Fail(NoRefreshValue)` (fixed `:363`); otherwise, still under that lock, it writes a rotated refresh value to the vault and keeps the access value (the rotation order of D6 is unchanged: write first, then keep). **Why the vault write runs under the lock:** without it a `Forget` can run between the check and the write, and the vault then holds a refresh value after `Forget` returned. **Cost:** while a rotating refresh writes, or `Forget` deletes, other callers of THAT serial wait on its lock for the vault call (the vault interface is synchronous; UNMEASURED with a real store). **Why `NoRefreshValue`:** after `Forget` the serial has no refresh value, and that is what its next request reports anyway. **Alternatives:** re-delete after a late write (cost: a window where the vault holds the value after `Forget` returned); `RefreshFailed` (cost: a caller retries a device that has been forgotten). **Not changed (named limit):** `StoreRefreshValue` during a flight is still followed by that flight's result (round 1's limit, B1869 G09). Cell T14 (two rows).
- **D23 THE CREDENTIAL CACHE NEVER UNDOES `Forget` OR `Issue` (Tuesday's ruling 2; Q23).** `CallbackCredentials` gains `_generations` (per serial, fixed `CallbackCredentials.cs:27`), changed only under `_sync` by `Issue` (after its vault write, beside the cache write, fixed `:64`) and `Forget` (fixed `:98`). `Forget` deletes the vault record FIRST (fixed `:90`) and then, under `_sync`, zeroes and drops the cached hash and bumps the generation; a vault failure propagates and leaves the credential as it was (HEAD1 cleared the cache first). `StoredHash` captures the generation under `_sync` before its unlocked vault read; after the read, under `_sync`: if the generation moved (fixed `:187`) the read is never cached and the answer is what the cache now holds (the new hash after `Issue`, nothing after `Forget`); else the first fill wins. **Why a generation and not one lock across the read (the commission's other default):** `_sync` is instance-wide, so holding it across a vault read would make every check wait behind one slow read, and an unauthenticated caller can force reads with unknown serials (B1869 F2); a per-serial lock map would grow with every serial a stranger names, while `_generations` grows only on `Issue` and `Forget` (host calls). **Cost:** one dictionary entry per serial ever issued or forgotten by the instance. **Alternative:** a per-serial lock held across the read (cost above). Cells A06 forget, A06 rotation, A07.
- **D24 `BodyFailed` (Tuesday's ruling 3; Q24).** In `HandleAsync`'s bounded read, after the `BodyTimedOut` catch: `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested) { throw; }` and `catch (Exception) { stop.Cancel(); return Result(Oxpd2CallbackOutcome.BodyFailed); }` (fixed `Oxpd2CallbackHandler.cs:152`), so a stream's `IOException`, its own `OperationCanceledException` (the caller's token not cancelled), `ObjectDisposedException` and `TimeoutException` (the reading task faulted, so the `BodyTimedOut` filter does not take it) each end `BodyFailed`; `BoundedReader` has zeroed its buffer in its `finally` (unchanged); the parse, the guard and the sink are after the read, so no sequence is recorded and the sink is not called; the result is `Result(BodyFailed)` (no body, no refusal fields, no exception text). **The outcome is APPENDED** after `SinkFailed` (fixed `Oxpd2CallbackOutcome.cs:46`; outcome count 13 -> 14), so no existing value is renumbered; D20's list and the README end `..., SinkFailed, BodyFailed`. **Alternative:** place it after `BodyTimedOut` (cost: renumbers five values a host might have stored). D20 and the README now say only `ArgumentNullException` and the caller's own cancellation leave `HandleAsync` (true: D24 closes the one gap). Cell B06 (four rows).
- **D25 A GRANT COPIES ITS VALUES (Tuesday's ruling 4; Q25).** `SolutionTokenGrant`'s constructor copies the access value (`accessValue.ToArray()`, fixed `SolutionTokenGrant.cs:16`), checks the COPY (zeroing it before it throws), and copies the new refresh value too (fixed `:32`). **The refresh copy goes one step past the ruling's words** ("cannot reach a send"): the same property for the vault write; default include it. **Alternative:** copy the access value only (cost: a caller's later change of the refresh buffer reaches the vault). Cell T15 (copy rows and the six bad values of B1868 G12 / B1869 G11).
- **D26 THE TEXT MADE TRUE (Tuesday's ruling 4; Q26).** README (`1-code.diff`, 20 added and 9 removed lines): the refresh-reuse bullet (HEAD1 `README.md:21-22`) says a request that waited uses that refresh's result, its access value or its failure, and refreshes for itself only when none completed after its wait began; two new bullets in Part 1 (a grant copies and checks; `Forget` during a refresh stores nothing); Part 2's `Issue`/`Forget` sentence adds "Each takes effect for every check that starts after it returns"; HEAD1 `:37`'s "so the same work is done" becomes "so the same comparison is done" plus "The lookup is not the same work: a serial with no cached hash reads the vault on every call, while a known one is served from memory, so whether a serial is enrolled may be told by timing (the serial is not a secret)"; the read step names `BodyFailed`; the outcomes list adds `BodyFailed` and the sentence on what `HandleAsync` throws; the Limits line HEAD1 `:95` ("A body stream that throws an I/O error passes that exception ...") is removed. `CallbackCredentials`'s class summary says the same as the README. No README line of the diff is wider than 118 characters (MEASURED by `awk 'length > 118'` over the fixed README: only round-1 lines).
