## HEAVY (other seats and reviews share this machine and ONE lock)
A heavy job = `dotnet restore`, `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, `dotnet publish`. **Every heavy job goes through `<lane>/gate/heavy.py`, in the foreground, ONE at a time; you never start `dotnet` any other way.** A job is `python3 -I '<lane>/gate/heavy.py' '<lane>' 'B1873 seat X25' <label> '<clone>' [K=V ...] -- dotnet <arguments>` (the argument order of the runner's own usage line: lane folder, owner word, label, working folder; a new label for every job, because the label names the job's log file; the locked restore of Step 5 passes `CI=true` before the `--`). What the runner does (its header): (1) reads load1 FIRST and waits while it is 7 or more (at most 30 minutes, then `BLOCKED: load`); (2) THEN takes the wave's lock by `mkdir` of EXACTLY `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/!CODING/Datasec/OXPd_2/.tools/wave1-heavy.lock.d` and writes `owner.txt`; (3) runs the ONE command under a time bound (900 s by default); (4) releases the lock by MOVING the folder to `<lane>/_quarantine/locks/` (never `rm`, never `rmdir`). **Waits are expected: other seats share this lock; allow 30 minutes of waits in all.** A lock held by another seat for more than 30 minutes ends the runner with `BLOCKED: lock held <its owner line>`: that line is your last line, and Tuesday treats it as a relaunch, not a finding; an `owner.txt` is never yours to break. **The drafter ran this runner unchanged (sha256 `31adedc8...`) for every dotnet job of its prototype (MEASURED, its `logs/gate.log`: every job `lock taken after 0 waits`); the round-1 seat and both readers ran it unchanged (RELAYED).** Never hold the lock while you read, edit or wait on a hosted run.

